### 2. Post-Quantum Cryptography Recommendations for Key Fragmentation in Low-Power Device Protocols
- **Presentation:** Ramadi Chirmananda presented [Post-Quantum Cryptography Recommendations for Key Fragmentation in Low-Power Device Protocols](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dispatch-post-quantum-cryptography-recommendations-for-key-fragmentation-in-low-power-device-protocols-00).
- **Overview:** Large post-quantum algorithm keys (e.g., ML-KEM) exceed the frame size of low-power link-layer protocols (e.g., 6LoWPAN MTU of 127 bytes). The presentation highlighted privacy and performance concerns when fragmenting sensitive key material across link-layer or network-layer fragmentation mechanisms.
- **Discussion:**
  - Eric Rescorla stated that generic, cross-protocol fragmentation guidance is ineffective because key transport and fragmentation issues are tightly bound to specific protocol mechanisms (e.g., DTLS stateless handshakes, anti-amplification limits, or 6LoWPAN mesh headers).
  - Participants agreed in chat and at the mic that key handling and fragmentation guidance must be addressed within the individual protocol working groups handling post-quantum transitions.
- **Outcome:** Dispatched as **No Action** in DISPATCH. Proponents should work directly with specific protocol working groups (e.g., 6LoWPAN, TLS/DTLS).

