### 3. The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data Encodings
- **Presentation:** Julian Reschke presented [The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data Encodings](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-dispatch-the-base16-base32-and-base64-data-encodings-00).
- **Overview:** RFC 4648 is currently a Proposed Standard with several open errata. Author Simon Josefsson created an experimental revision (`draft-josefsson-rfc4648bis`). The proposal considers resolving errata, updating references, refactoring common text, advancing the specification to Full Standard (STD), and potentially defining explicit named profiles (e.g., strict parsing, Base64URL without padding).
- **Discussion:**
  - Phillip Hallam-Baker, Bron Gondwana, Rich Salz, and Justin Richer supported keeping the document focused on cleanup, resolving errata, and consolidating common variants (such as Base64URL without padding) into one clear reference.
  - Colin Perkins, Mike Bishop, and Area Director Andrew Newton noted that if the scope extends beyond simple errata cleanup to making normative profile recommendations or deprecating variants, AD sponsorship is inappropriate because consensus must be formally judged.
  - Bron Gondwana and others suggested a full working group might be process heavy, but ADs expressed reluctance to sponsor normative changes without a dedicated review venue.
- **Outcome:** Create a dedicated **mailing list** to resolve errata, evaluate scope, and determine whether to charter a small, tightly scoped working group or pursue AD sponsorship for an editorial update.

